# 🤖 System Prompt

```java
.defaultSystem(SYSTEM_PROMPT)
```

Registers a **default system prompt** for every request.

### Why?

Instead of writing

```java
.system(...)
```

for every request,

configure it once:

```java
ChatClient.Builder
        .defaultSystem(...)
        .build();
```

Now every prompt automatically follows the same rules.

---

# 💬 Chat Endpoint

```java
GET /api/chat
```

Uses

```java
.call()
```

### Flow

```text
User

↓

Controller

↓

ChatClient

↓

Azure OpenAI

↓

Complete Response
```

Since `.call()` is **blocking**, the browser waits until the entire response is generated.

---

# ⚡ Streaming Endpoint

```java
GET /api/stream
```

Uses

```java
.stream()
```

Returns

```java
Flux<String>
```

instead of a single `String`.

---

# Why Streaming?

Without Streaming (`.call()`)

```text
User
   │
Wait...
Wait...
Wait...
Complete Response
```

The response appears only after the AI finishes generating everything.

---

With Streaming (`.stream()`)

```text
User
   │
Hello...
This...
is...
Spring...
AI...
```

The response appears **token by token**, just like **ChatGPT**.

---

# SSE (Server-Sent Events)

```java
produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE
```

Tells Spring Boot to stream each token immediately to the browser.

Without it:

❌ Wait until the complete response is ready.

With it:

✅ Live streaming.

---

# ChatClient Flow

```text
User Input

↓

ChatClient

↓

Default System Prompt

↓

User Prompt

↓

Azure OpenAI

↓

AI Response
```

---

# Blocking vs Streaming

| `.call()`                 | `.stream()`             |
| ------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Blocking                  | Non-blocking            |
| Returns `String`          | Returns `Flux<String>`  |
| Complete response at once | Response token by token |
| Simple APIs               | ChatGPT-like UI         |

